The 486 (gen 4) answers CPUID but doesn't let EFLAGS.ID be set, the bit software checks before using CPUID. A
program that checks the flag takes the CPU for an early 486 without CPUID and runs that path's chipset probes
instead (a CPU detection library of the 1990s probes port 22h with interrupts off there, and returns with them
still off). It is now a 486 of the kind that had CPUID (486DX4, the SL-enhanced 486s, the Am486s): the flag can be
set, and CPUID's feature bits are the 486's, the FPU only: no time-stamp counter or CMPXCHG8B, which came with the
Pentium (gen 5).

diff --git a/i386.c b/i386.c
index bd653cc..59df619 100644
--- a/i386.c
+++ b/i386.c
@@ -2568,7 +2568,7 @@ static bool call_isr(CPUI386 *cpu, int no, bool pusherr, int ext);
 	}
 
 #define EFLAGS_MASK_386 0x37fd7
-#define EFLAGS_MASK_486 0x77fd7
+#define EFLAGS_MASK_486 0x277fd7 /* ID: the 486s with CPUID let it be set */
 #define EFLAGS_MASK_586 0x277fd7
 #define EFLAGS_MASK (cpu->flags_mask)
 
@@ -3866,7 +3866,7 @@ static bool verrw_helper(CPUI386 *cpu, int sel, int wr, int *zf)
 	case 1: \
 		REGi(0) = 0 | (0 << 4) | (cpu->gen << 8); \
 		REGi(3) = 0; \
-		REGi(2) = 0x110; \
+		REGi(2) = cpu->gen > 4 ? 0x110 : 0; /* TSC, CX8: 586+ */ \
 		REGi(1) = 0; \
 		if (cpu->fpu) REGi(2) |= 1; \
 		if (cpu->gen > 5) REGi(2) |= 0x8820; \
